---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import eqEightKick from "../images/eq-eight-kick.png";
import eqEightSub from "../images/eq-eight-sub.png";
import joshBakerKickVariation from "../images/josh-baker-kick-variation.png";
import reverb from "../images/reverb.png";
---

<div>
	<main>
		<h1 class="ml-4 text-6xl font-thin">sevilla</h1>
		<div class="m-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
			<p>arrangement</p>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Intro</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(32 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					The most functional section of the track. Typically just
					kick, hi-hats, clap/snare, and maybe a subtle percussion
					loop. No bassline, no melody, no vocals. The goal is to give
					a DJ a clean, neutral 32 bars to work with. If you're making
					a track for DJs specifically, never shortchange the intro —
					a 16 bar intro forces the DJ to rush their mix. Think of it
					as a handshake between your track and the DJ — the more room
					you give them, the more they'll love playing your record. A
					common technique is to have your kick and hi-hat pattern
					locked in from bar one so the incoming DJ has a clear
					rhythmic reference to sync against. Some producers also add
					a very subtle, almost subliminal pad underneath just to hint
					at the key of the track without giving anything away.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Buildup / Rise</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(16 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					This is where you start introducing the identity of your
					track. The bassline usually enters here, maybe a synth stab
					or chord stab, and you begin hinting at the main melodic
					elements without fully revealing them. A lot of producers
					use a high-pass filter sweeping open across these 16 bars to
					create a sense of things arriving. Tension is the whole
					point here — you're making a promise to the dancefloor that
					something is coming. A useful Ableton technique is to
					automate a filter cutoff on your main synth or bass so it
					gradually opens up across the full 16 bars, giving a
					physical sense of forward motion. You can also start
					introducing your clap or snare here if it wasn't in the
					intro, which adds to the sense of the track waking up. Think
					of these 16 bars as a runway — by the time you hit bar 16
					the listener should feel like they absolutely need whatever
					comes next.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Main Section</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(32-64 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					Everything is in. Kick, bass, melody, vocals if you have
					them, percussion layers, all of it. This is the body of your
					track and where the groove needs to be airtight. Most
					producers split this into two 16 bar loops with subtle
					variations — maybe a percussion element drops out for 8
					bars, or a synth line shifts — to keep it from feeling
					static. The longer your main section, the more internal
					variation it needs. In Ableton, a good approach is to build
					your core 8 bar loop first until it feels undeniable, then
					duplicate it and start adding and subtracting elements to
					create the variation across 32 or 64 bars. Pay close
					attention to your low end here — your kick and bass need to
					work together rhythmically and tonally, which means making
					sure your bassline notes are tuned to the root or fifth of
					your key. The main section is also where a DJ is most likely
					to be mixing over you, so having a strong, consistent groove
					that doesn't shift too dramatically makes their job easier
					and makes your track more mixable.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Breakdown</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(16-32 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					Drums disappear, bass disappears, and you're left with the
					emotional core of the track — pads, chords, maybe a filtered
					or reverb-soaked vocal. This section lives or dies on your
					chord progression and atmosphere. 16 bars feels tight and
					punchy, 32 bars gives more room to breathe and makes the
					drop hit harder when it comes. This is where the track stops
					being a groove and becomes a feeling — it's the moment that
					makes people close their eyes on the dancefloor. In Ableton,
					heavy reverb and long delay tails are your best friends here
					— let your pad chords ring out and blur into each other to
					create a sense of weightlessness and space. If you have a
					vocal, this is where you want it most exposed, maybe running
					it through a low-pass filter or adding a tape-style delay to
					give it an intimate, dreamlike quality. A subtle trick is to
					let a very quiet sub bass hum underneath just to keep the
					body of the room engaged even while the groove is gone — the
					crowd won't consciously hear it but they'll feel the floor.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Drop</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(4-8 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					The moment of re-entry after the breakdown. The drums and
					bass slam back in at full energy and the impact is entirely
					emotional — it's the payoff for all the tension built during
					the breakdown. In house music the drop tends to be less
					dramatic than in EDM, but it still needs to land with
					weight. Some producers add a single bar of silence or a
					snare roll right before it to make the re-entry hit even
					harder. In Ableton, try automating a short volume dip or a
					low-pass filter closing right at the very end of the
					breakdown — even just one beat of near-silence before the
					kick returns makes the drop feel like a physical impact. The
					drop is also a great place to introduce a new percussive
					element or a subtle new texture that wasn't in the first
					main section, planting a seed for what the second main
					section will develop. Keep the drop itself relatively simple
					and clean — this is not the moment to introduce complexity,
					it's the moment to let the rhythm do all the work.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Second Main Section</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(32-64 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					After the drop you're back into the full groove, but this
					should feel like an elevated version of your first main
					section. The listener has been through the breakdown and the
					emotional moment, so the same groove hits differently now. A
					lot of producers introduce something new here that wasn't in
					the first main section — a new synth layer, a counter
					melody, or a vocal ad lib — to signal that this is the peak
					of the track rather than just a repeat. In Ableton, a simple
					but effective approach is to take your first main section
					session clips and duplicate them, then layer one new element
					on top that adds brightness or energy — a high synth line,
					an extra shaker, or a vocal chop loop works well. This is
					also the section where you can afford to be a little more
					aggressive with your percussion, adding more layers or
					pushing the hi-hat pattern to add urgency. Think of the
					second main section as the track making good on every
					promise it made in the buildup and breakdown — it should
					feel like the fullest, richest version of your groove.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">
					<span class="italic">Outro</span>
					<span class="text-emerald-500">(32 bars)</span>
				</p>
				<p>
					The mirror image of your intro. Elements drop away one by
					one until you're back to just drums and maybe a faint pad.
					The bassline is usually the last melodic element to leave.
					Same logic as the intro — give the next DJ 32 clean bars to
					work with. A short outro is one of the most frustrating
					things a producer can do to a DJ. In Ableton, a clean way to
					build your outro is to literally reverse-engineer your intro
					— take the same clips and automate elements muting one by
					one every 8 bars so the strip-down feels gradual and
					intentional rather than abrupt. Be careful with reverb tails
					here — if a pad or synth has a long reverb decay, make sure
					it fades out fully before your final bars or it will bleed
					into the next DJ's track in an ugly way. Ending on a
					perfectly clean 8 bars of just kick and hi-hat is a gift to
					whoever is playing after you.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="m-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
			<p>tracks/elements</p>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">Kick</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					Learnt how to make the kick more interesting from a <a
						href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qlk89LDp4KQ"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer">Josh Baker Syntho video</a
					>
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="m-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
			<p class="mb-2">groove</p>
			<p>
				Groove in Ableton is a system for injecting rhythmic feel and
				humanization into your clips by subtly shifting the timing and
				velocity of notes or audio warp markers away from the rigid
				grid. Rather than every hit landing exactly on the beat, groove
				introduces micro-timing variations — the kind that make a drum
				pattern feel like it was played by a human rather than
				programmed by a machine.
			</p>
			<p>
				The Groove Pool is the central hub where you manage all of this:
				it holds a collection of groove presets (either from Ableton's
				built-in library or extracted directly from audio clips using
				the "Extract Groove" function), and lets you dial in how much of
				that feel is applied via parameters like Timing, Random,
				Velocity, and Quantize amount.
			</p>
			<p>
				You can apply a groove to multiple clips at once and then
				fine-tune the intensity globally from the pool, making it a
				powerful tool for locking your drums, bass, and melodic elements
				to the same rhythmic pocket — or for giving each element its own
				sense of feel.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="m-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
			<p>audio effects</p>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">"Drum Buss"</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					Drum Buss is a one-stop drum processing unit in Ableton that
					adds punch, warmth, and character to percussive material. It
					combines several tools in one device: Drive and Crunch for
					saturation and grit, Boom for low-end weight with a tunable
					frequency and decay, a transient shaper for controlling the
					snap of hits, and a high-frequency damping control to tame
					harshness. Because all these controls are tuned specifically
					for drums, it gives you a fast and musical workflow without
					needing to stack multiple separate devices.
				</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					Its primary use case is on a drum group or bus channel,
					where it helps glue all your drum elements together and
					makes the kit feel cohesive. On individual tracks it becomes
					more selective — it works well on kicks that need sub
					weight, snares that need more crack, and claps that need
					bite, but it adds little value on hi-hats and cymbals where
					it can introduce mud and harshness instead.
				</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					On individual drum elements, Drum Buss works best in
					conjunction with EQ Eight rather than in place of it. The
					recommended signal chain is EQ Eight first, then Drum Buss —
					you shape and clean up the frequency content first, then let
					Drum Buss add character and dynamics to an already
					well-sculpted sound. This way the saturation and Boom are
					working on something focused rather than amplifying unwanted
					frequencies.
				</p>
				<p>
					The key to knowing whether you actually need Drum Buss on a
					given element is to listen in context with the full track
					after you've done your EQ work, rather than soloing the
					element in isolation. Ask yourself whether the kick hits
					hard enough, whether the snare cuts through, whether the
					clap slaps. If something still feels like it's missing
					punch, weight, or character after EQ, that's your cue to
					reach for Drum Buss. If it already sounds right, leave it
					alone — unnecessary processing is how mixes lose clarity.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">"Reverb"</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					Reverb is essentially a simulation of physical space. When
					sound is produced in any environment, whether a small
					bathroom or a large concert hall, it bounces off the walls
					and surfaces and reaches your ears as a series of echoes
					that blend together into what we perceive as the "room."
					Ableton's Reverb effect recreates this phenomenon
					artificially, allowing you to place any sound inside a
					simulated space. For vocal samples specifically, reverb is
					one of the most important tools you have. A dry, unprocessed
					vocal can sound flat and disconnected from the rest of your
					mix, and a touch of reverb gives it a sense of air and space
					that makes it feel like it belongs in the track.
				</p>
				<p class="mb-2">
					In house music you generally want to be careful not to
					overdo it on vocals, a subtle short reverb keeps the vocal
					present and upfront while still sounding natural. For other
					elements, reverb works beautifully on things like synth pads
					to make them feel wide and atmospheric, on snares and claps
					to give them a sense of size and punch, and on percussive
					elements to add depth without pushing them too far back in
					the mix.
				</p>
				<Image src={reverb} alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs." />
				<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Input Filter - </span>This
						determines which frequencies of your signal get fed into
						the reverb before it does anything. The Lo Cut removes
						low frequencies from entering the reverb, which is
						something you almost always want to do because bass
						frequencies in a reverb tail quickly make a mix sound
						muddy and undefined. The Hi Cut removes high frequencies
						from entering the reverb, which can soften and warm the
						reverb's character. Think of it as a gate that shapes
						what the reverb is actually working with before it
						processes anything.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Early Reflections - </span>These
						are the very first echoes that occur when sound hits the
						nearest surfaces in a room. They arrive at your ears
						quickly and distinctly before the main reverb tail
						develops. The Amount controls how loud these initial
						reflections are, the Rate controls how fast they
						modulate if Spin is enabled, and Shape affects the
						overall character and density of those first
						reflections. In practical terms, early reflections
						contribute a lot to how you perceive the size and
						immediacy of a space. A room feels small when early
						reflections arrive quickly and closely together.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Diffusion Network - </span>This
						is the main body of the reverb, the long tail that
						follows the early reflections. The graph controls how
						different frequencies decay over time, so you can have
						high frequencies fade away faster than low ones or vice
						versa. Diffusion controls how smooth or grainy the tail
						sounds, higher diffusion means a smoother and more
						blended tail while lower diffusion can sound more
						textured and complex. Scale affects the overall size of
						the diffusion pattern.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Predelay - </span>This is the
						gap in milliseconds between the dry signal playing and
						the reverb tail beginning. Even a small predelay of a
						few milliseconds can do a lot for clarity, because it
						lets the original sound hit your ears cleanly before the
						reverb washes in behind it. This is particularly
						important on vocals and snares where you need the
						transient, the initial attack of the sound, to remain
						sharp and defined.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Size - </span>Controls how large
						the simulated space feels. A small size sounds like a
						tight room or chamber, a large size sounds like a
						cathedral or arena. This works hand in hand with Decay.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Decay - </span>This is how long
						the reverb tail rings out before it fades to silence,
						measured in seconds. Short decay times feel intimate and
						tight, long decay times feel expansive and atmospheric.
						For a house music bassline you want virtually no reverb
						or an extremely short decay. For pads and atmospheres a
						longer decay can sound beautiful.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Stereo - </span>Controls how
						wide the reverb is in the stereo field. Higher values
						spread the reverb further left and right, which can add
						a real sense of width and space to your mix.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Density - </span>Controls how
						many individual reflections make up the reverb tail.
						Higher density sounds smoother and more lush, lower
						density sounds more sparse and you can sometimes hear
						individual echoes within the tail.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Dry/Wet - </span>The balance
						between the original unprocessed signal and the reverb
						effect. When Reverb is placed directly on an instrument
						or vocal track, you typically want this fairly low,
						somewhere between 15 and 30%, so the source sound stays
						upfront. When used on a dedicated send/return track,
						which is the more common and flexible approach in
						mixing, you set it to 100% wet because the dry signal is
						already coming through its own channel.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						<span class="font-bold">Chorus - </span>Adds a subtle
						modulation to the reverb tail which gives it a slightly
						animated, shimmering quality. The Amount and Rate
						controls determine how intense and how fast that
						modulation is. Keeping both values low gives a gentle,
						natural movement. Pushing them higher can make the
						reverb feel more lush but also less realistic.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p class="mb-2">"Compressor"</p>
				<p>
					A compressor is a tool that automatically turns down the
					volume of a sound when it gets too loud, and then lets it
					come back up again. Think of it like an invisible hand on a
					volume knob — when the sound hits a certain level it pulls
					the volume down, and when the sound drops below that level
					it lets the volume back up. This keeps your sounds
					controlled, tight, and sitting nicely in the mix without
					jumping out unexpectedly.
				</p>
				<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
					<p class="mb-2">Sidechain</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Sidechaining takes the compressor a step further.
						Instead of the compressor reacting to the sound it is
						sitting on, it reacts to a completely different sound.
						In house music the most common use is sidechaining the
						sub bass to the kick drum — this means every time the
						kick hits, the compressor on the sub automatically ducks
						the sub's volume down for a brief moment, then lets it
						come back up. This is what creates that classic pumping,
						breathing low end sound that is the backbone of house
						music.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						The reason sidechaining the kick and sub together is so
						important is because they both live in the same low
						frequency range. Without sidechaining they will
						constantly clash and fight each other, making the low
						end sound boomy, muddy, and indistinct. The sidechain
						essentially gives the kick the right of way every time
						it hits, so the kick always punches through clearly
						while the sub fills in the space in between.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						To set up the sidechain in Ableton, place a Compressor
						on your sub bass track. Click the arrow on the left side
						of the compressor to expand the sidechain section, then
						turn on External and select your kick track as the
						source. This tells the compressor to listen to the kick
						drum and react to it rather than reacting to the sub
						itself.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Threshold is the level at which the compressor kicks in
						— in the screenshot this was set to -25.2 dB. The lower
						the threshold the earlier the compressor starts
						reacting. For sidechain compression on a sub you want
						the compressor to react every time the kick hits, so set
						the threshold low enough that every kick hit triggers
						it. Watch the GR (Gain Reduction) meter and aim for
						around -6 to -8 dB of gain reduction — this means the
						sub is ducking by that amount every time the kick hits,
						which is the sweet spot for a tight house music low end.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						The SC Filter controls what frequency the compressor
						listens to inside the sidechain signal before it reacts.
						Setting the SC Filter Freq to 80 Hz tells the compressor
						to focus on the low frequency thud of the kick rather
						than its full signal including the click and high
						frequency content — this makes the sidechain more
						precise and musical. Leave the Res at 0.71 and SC Gain
						at 0.00 dB as these do not need any adjustment.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Ratio controls how aggressively the compressor turns the
						volume down once it kicks in. A ratio of 4:1 as seen in
						the screenshot is a good starting point for sidechain
						compression — it means for every 4 dB the sub goes over
						the threshold, the compressor only lets 1 dB through,
						creating a firm and controlled duck without being too
						extreme.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Attack controls how fast the compressor reacts when the
						kick hits. At 1.00 ms as seen in the screenshot, the
						compressor grabs the sub almost instantly when the kick
						hits, which is exactly what you want — it lets the
						kick's initial punch come through clearly and
						immediately ducks the sub out of the way.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Release controls how fast the sub comes back up after
						the kick has passed. At 30 ms as seen in the screenshot
						this is set quite fast, meaning the sub bounces back
						quickly and naturally after each kick hit. This is what
						creates that rhythmic pumping feel — if the release is
						too slow the sub will stay ducked for too long and the
						low end will feel hollow and empty between kicks.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						The Golden Rule for sidechain compression is the same as
						EQ — always set it up with your full mix playing. The
						kick and sub need to be heard together so you can feel
						the relationship between them. When the sidechain is set
						correctly the low end should feel tight, punchy, and
						energetic — the kick hits hard and the sub breathes in
						and out around it in a way that drives the groove
						forward.
					</p>
					<Image
						src={eqEightSub}
						alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs."
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
				<p>"EQ Eight"</p>
				<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
					<p class="mb-2">Kick</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Every kick drum sample is different, so before touching
						any knobs, turn on the spectrum analyzer in EQ Eight and
						play the kick so you can see where its energy is sitting
						— this gray moving shape tells you everything about
						where the kick lives in the frequency spectrum.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						EQ Eight has two filter types you will use for the kick.
						The first is the High-Pass filter, which looks like a
						ramp sloping upward from left to right — this cuts
						everything below the frequency you set, letting
						everything above pass through. The second is the Bell
						filter, which looks like a diamond shape — this boosts
						or cuts a specific frequency while leaving everything
						around it relatively untouched.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 1 — Remove the Rumble. Set the filter type to
						High-Pass (the ramp icon, first shape on the left in the
						filter row) and place it at 30 Hz. This removes ultra
						low frequencies that you cannot even hear but silently
						eat up headroom and make your mix sound weak and
						cluttered. No Gain or Q adjustment needed here, just set
						the frequency and the filter does the rest.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 2 — Boost the Body and Thud. Set the filter type to
						Bell (the diamond icon). Look at the spectrum analyzer
						and find where the biggest peak is — this is your kick's
						fundamental tone and where its power lives. For most
						house kicks this sits somewhere between 60–100 Hz, so
						place Band 2 there and add a gentle boost of +2 to +3 dB
						with a Q of around 1.0. Think of this as turning up the
						punch of the kick, but keep it subtle as a little goes a
						long way here.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 3 — Cut the Mud. Set the filter type to Bell (the
						diamond icon). This is the zone that makes kicks sound
						boxy, hollow, and cheap if there is too much of it.
						Listen carefully for a cardboard-like or tubby tone and
						place Band 3 somewhere in the 200–400 Hz range, cutting
						by -3 to -4 dB with a Q of around 1.5 to keep the cut
						focused and surgical. Think of this as removing the
						cheap hollow sound and replacing it with something tight
						and solid.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 4 — Add the Click and Attack. Set the filter type
						to Bell (the diamond icon). Place Band 4 around 3–4 kHz
						and add a gentle boost of +2 to +3 dB with a Q of around
						1.0 to bring out the knock and attack of the kick. This
						is what makes your kick audible on small speakers,
						earbuds, and phones. If your kick already sounds punchy
						and cuts through naturally, go easy here or skip it
						entirely — always let your ears decide.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						The Golden Rule — always do all of this with your full
						mix playing, never in solo. The kick needs to work
						alongside your bass, sub, and every other element, and
						the only way to know if your EQ is working is to hear
						everything together at the same time.
					</p>
					<Image
						src={eqEightKick}
						alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs."
					/>
				</div>
				<div class="my-4 p-4 border rounded-r-xl">
					<p class="mb-2">Sub</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Just like the kick, every sub bass is different, so
						before touching any knobs, turn on the spectrum analyzer
						in EQ Eight and play the sub so you can see where its
						energy is sitting — a sub sine will show most of its
						energy concentrated very low, typically below 100 Hz,
						which is exactly where it should live.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						EQ Eight has three filter types you will use for the
						sub. The High-Pass filter which looks like a ramp
						sloping upward from left to right, the Bell filter which
						looks like a diamond shape, and the Low-Pass filter
						which looks like a ramp sloping downward from left to
						right — this is the opposite of the high-pass and cuts
						everything above the frequency you set.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 1 — Remove the Rumble. Set the filter type to
						High-Pass (the ramp sloping upward) and place it at 30
						Hz. Just like the kick, this removes ultra low
						frequencies that you cannot hear but waste headroom and
						clutter your mix. No Gain or Q adjustment needed here,
						just set the frequency and the filter does the rest.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 2 — Boost the Fundamental. Set the filter type to
						Bell (the diamond icon). A sub sine lives lower than a
						kick, so look at the spectrum analyzer and find the
						biggest peak — for most sub basses this sits around
						80–100 Hz. Place Band 2 right at that peak and add a
						gentle boost of +2 to +3 dB with a Q of around 1.0 to
						reinforce the core weight and tone of the sub. Let the
						spectrum analyzer guide exactly where to place it rather
						than using a fixed number every time.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						Band 3 — Low-Pass Filter. This is the most important
						band for the sub. Set the filter type to Low-Pass (the
						ramp sloping downward) and place it at around 150–200
						Hz. This cuts everything above that point so the sub
						stays locked in its own low frequency lane and does not
						compete with your bassline, pads, or any other elements
						that live higher up in the spectrum. No Gain or Q
						adjustment needed here, just set the frequency and the
						filter does the rest. Note that you only need 3 bands
						for the sub — do not activate any further bands as they
						will be redundant and unnecessary.
					</p>
					<p class="mb-2">
						The Golden Rule — always do all of this with your full
						mix playing, never in solo. The sub needs to work
						alongside your kick above everything else, and if you
						have a sidechain compressor set up from the kick to the
						sub, the EQ and the sidechain work together to keep the
						low end tight, punchy, and clean.
					</p>
					<Image
						src={eqEightSub}
						alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs."
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
</div>
